## What I changed

The defect, verified at HEAD before editing: read_first_scored returns (map, usize) where the usize is non-blank lines in .yoyo/risk_first_scored.jsonl that failed to parse — documented in parse_first_scored as "returned, not swallowed" (#764's fix, one ledger over). Both consumers in src/commands_risk_unhittable.rs (:309, :760) bound it to _dropped and threw it away. A malformed line yields no path/ts, so first_scored_age returns None for whatever path it named — landing the surprise in unmeasurable, the identical state of a path the scorer has never seen. A corrupted ledger and a clean one printed byte-identical output on both seams.

The fix (one file, `src/commands_risk_unhittable.rs` + its test module):
• `dropped: usize on both UnhittableCount (live) and RetrospectiveCount (retrospective); usize to match the producer and ValidationLedger::Present`.
• Both _dropped bindings → dropped, set on the count. The pure joins were deliberately not touched — they take (surprises, ts, &map, founding) and cannot see a dropped line, because a dropped line is not a row. It belongs to the reading.
• **One `dropped_ledger_clause(dropped) -> Option<String>` consumed by both** notes — not two copies that agree today. Appended on the existing "; " separator, ASCII hyphen, no new glyph.
• `None at dropped == 0: that's the whole regression surface, pinned with whole-string assert_eq!` on both seams.

Tests (written first, and the reader is tested rather than the field): a tempdir ledger of one valid line plus one not-json line run through retrospective_unhittable_at; the tie this field exists to break (a truncated record makes a surprise undecidable and dropped > 0) with a clean-ledger control reading the same event as decidable and unhittable; anti-vacuous assertions on the hostile bytes.

Positive control, run serially as one atomic mutate→run→restore: neutering the clause reddened exactly the 5 expected tests by name, near-miss guards stayed green, restore returned 59/59, grep -c NEUTERED = 0.

Verification on the committed tree: cargo fmt -- --check PASS, cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings clean, cargo test 5815 passed / 0 failed. This repo's own ledger measures dropped = 0 by an independent Python count, so the byte-identity case is the live one here.

